<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Defs>
  <!--
    What the ground under an aircraft on our own flight does to it as it lifts off or touches down (Framework 0.158.0; the owner's decision
    of 18 Nov 2026, docs/development/vehicle-osprey.md, "What it lands on"). The first loaded instance wins; another mod may patch it. Every
    figure is authored for play. Not yet tested in game.
  -->
  <Phobos.VehicleSystems.PhobosLandingSurfaceRulesDef>
    <defName>PhobosLandingSurfaceRules</defName>

    <!-- Loose, dry soil or sand under it: each engine loses half a percent of its health at each lift-off or touch-down, so about seventy
         dusty trips (a lift-off and a touch-down each) take an engine from new to the floor below, unless it is repaired. The real basis: an Osprey's engine stalled on dust at
         Bellows, Hawaii, in 2015, and its engines lasted under 400 hours in service against the design's far longer life. Rolling on or off
         throws up half the dust (crews in brown-out land rolling where they can). An inlet barrier keeps four fifths of it out. -->
    <engineWearPerEvent>0.005</engineWearPerEvent>
    <!-- Dust never takes an engine below 30% of its health (Framework 0.163.0, fault-read L2 of 19 Nov 2026): the engine model counts an
         engine out at 20%, and both engines wear together, so without a floor a lift-off that was allowed could lose both a second later.
         Seventy dusty trips take a new engine to the floor; there it gives much less power until it is repaired. -->
    <engineWearFloor>0.3</engineWearFloor>
    <rollingFactor>0.5</rollingFactor>
    <barrierStops>0.8</barrierStops>

    <!-- Dry growth under it: a 4% chance at each lift-off or touch-down of a small fire, with growth under the whole aircraft, three times
         that in a heat wave. The real basis: an Osprey's exhaust set a grass fire at a North Carolina range in 2013. -->
    <fireChancePerEvent>0.04</fireChancePerEvent>
    <heatWaveFactor>3</heatWaveFactor>
    <fireSize>0.3</fireSize>

    <!-- Under more snow than this, ground is neither dusty nor dry. Rain falling makes all ground wet. -->
    <snowAtMost>0.2</snowAtMost>
    <!-- Open ground more than a quarter wet (the Framework's ground wetness, after the rain has stopped) is neither dusty nor dry:
         about seven hours after a soaking on a mild day. -->
    <wetAbove>0.25</wetAbove>
  </Phobos.VehicleSystems.PhobosLandingSurfaceRulesDef>
</Defs>
